      title: "Genesis 12",
      tourTitle: "The Call of Abram",
      hook: `God commands a seventy-five-year-old man to leave everything for a land he will be shown, and promises him descendants, blessing, and a great name.`,
      tour: `Genesis 12 is where the universal history of chapters 1-11 narrows to one man and one family, and everything changes. Read verses 1-3 as slowly as possible: the command (go from your country, your kindred, your father's house) and the triple promise (land, descendants, blessing) are the template the rest of the book — and arguably the rest of the Bible — works out. Then watch what Abram does: he goes, with no recorded hesitation, no question, no argument. The chapter then shows immediately that the promise is not a protection from difficulty. There is a famine in the land. Abram goes down to Egypt and passes Sarai off as his sister to save his own life, and Pharaoh's household is struck with plagues, and Pharaoh sends him away reproachfully. The call is followed by a famine and a moral failure. That is the pattern of the patriarchal narratives.`,
