      hook: `Rich herders' quarrels force Abram and Lot to divide the land; Lot chooses the fertile Jordan plain and pitches near Sodom.`,
      tour: `Genesis 13 is a chapter of magnanimity and geography. Abram and Lot have both become rich in livestock, and their herdsmen quarrel over pasture. Abram proposes they separate and gives Lot the first choice of land — a striking gesture from the one who holds the divine promise, since giving up land would seem to be giving up the promise. Lot looks east and sees the Jordan valley is well watered like the garden of the LORD, like Egypt — two loaded comparisons — and chooses it. He moves toward Sodom. The text notes that the men of Sodom were wicked, great sinners against the LORD, before moving on. Then after Lot departs God reaffirms the promise to Abram: lift up your eyes and look from the place where you are, northward and southward and eastward and westward, for all the land that you see I will give to you and to your offspring forever. Abram moves to Hebron and builds an altar.`,
