      hook: `Four kings defeat five, take Lot captive, and Abram pursues them with three hundred men — then the mysterious Melchizedek blesses the victor.`,
      tour: `Genesis 14 is the most politically and geographically complex chapter in the patriarchal narratives, and the one most difficult to place in historical context. A coalition of four Mesopotamian kings defeats five kings of the cities of the plain — Sodom, Gomorrah, Admah, Zeboiim, Bela — and takes Lot captive with his household. When Abram hears this he musters his trained men, pursues to Dan, divides his forces, defeats the kings, and recovers Lot and all the goods. What follows is theologically dense: Melchizedek, king of Salem and priest of El Elyon (God Most High), brings out bread and wine and blesses Abram. Abram gives him a tenth of everything. Then the king of Sodom offers Abram the goods, asking only for the people; Abram refuses to take even a thread or a sandal strap, so that the king cannot say he made Abram rich. The episode establishes Abram as a military figure and introduces the enigmatic Melchizedek, who will become an important type in Psalm 110 and the New Testament letter to the Hebrews.`,
