      title: "Genesis 15",
      tourTitle: "The Covenant of Pieces",
      hook: `God shows Abram the stars and promises offspring as numerous; Abram believes, and God formalizes the promise in a mysterious covenant ceremony.`,
      tour: `Genesis 15 is the chapter on which Paul will build an entire theology and on which the Reformation will turn, because of one verse: Abram believed the LORD, and he counted it to him as righteousness. Read the surrounding context — Abram has just been told to look at the stars and has asked, as he will in the next chapter, how can I know this is real? — to understand that the belief in verse 6 is not a moment of triumphant certainty but of commitment in the face of uncertainty. The covenant ceremony that follows, in which God alone passes between the divided animal halves while Abram sleeps in a deep and dreadful darkness, is one of the most archaic rituals in the Bible: in ancient Near Eastern treaty-making, passing between the pieces meant submitting to the curse of being cut apart if the covenant was broken. Here only God passes through. The covenant is unilateral. Then God names the terms: four hundred years of slavery in Egypt, and after that, a return.`,
