      tourTitle: "Hagar and Ishmael",
      hook: `Sarai offers her servant Hagar to Abram; Hagar conceives and despises Sarai; the angel of the LORD finds her in the wilderness and promises a son.`,
      tour: `Genesis 16 introduces Hagar, who is arguably the most surprising theological figure in the chapter. The human solution to the problem of barrenness — the mistress-surrogate system was legally recognized in ancient Near Eastern law, and the reader is not meant to be shocked by the proposal — produces the expected complications: Hagar conceives, Sarai is diminished in her eyes, Hagar is mistreated and flees. What happens in the wilderness is where the chapter becomes remarkable. The angel of the LORD finds Hagar — not Abraham, not Sarai — beside a spring of water, calls her by name, asks where she has come from and where she is going, and makes her a promise that echoes the promise to Abram: I will multiply your offspring so that they cannot be counted. The promise is not to Abram's heir but to a pregnant Egyptian slave running away from her mistress. Hagar names God — you are El-roi, the God who sees me — and her naming of God is the only instance in the Bible of a human being giving God a name.`,
