      hook: `The two angels arrive in Sodom, Lot receives them, the men of the city surround the house, and fire and sulfur fall from heaven at dawn.`,
      tour: `Genesis 19 is one of the most disturbing chapters in Genesis, and it rewards careful reading precisely because of its discomforts. Lot, the nephew of Abraham, is sitting in the gate of Sodom — the position of a respected citizen — and he receives the two angels with the same extravagant hospitality that Abraham showed in chapter 18. The contrast that follows is the moral of the chapter: the men of Sodom surround the house and demand Lot bring out the men to abuse them. Lot offers his daughters instead — a detail that modern readers find appalling and that should be read as evidence of both the extreme values of ancient hospitality and the extreme degradation the chapter is documenting. The angels strike the mob blind and tell Lot to take his family out. Lot hesitates. The angels seize him and his wife and daughters by the hand and bring them out: the mercy of the LORD is the explicit reason given. Lot's wife looks back and becomes a pillar of salt. Lot and his daughters end the chapter in a cave, and the incestuous origin of Moab and Ammon is given without editorial comment.`,
