      tour: `Genesis 20 is the second of three wife-sister episodes in Genesis (cf. 12:10-20 and 26:6-11) and the most theologically nuanced. Abraham is now in Gerar, in Philistine territory. He again says Sarah is his sister, and Abimelech the king takes her. The chapter then does something unusual: God speaks to Abimelech in a dream, tells him he is a dead man because the woman is someone's wife, and Abimelech protests his innocence, which God acknowledges. He has not touched her. God tells him to restore the woman to her husband, who is a prophet and will pray for him. The chapter asks the reader to notice that God's justice is not tribal: Abimelech acts with more integrity than Abraham in this episode, and God acknowledges it explicitly. Abraham's explanation for his behavior — she is indeed my sister, my father's daughter though not my mother's daughter — deepens the portrait of a flawed patriarch.`,
