      tourTitle: "Isaac Born, Hagar Sent Away",
      hook: `Sarah bears Isaac in her old age; the conflict between Ishmael and Isaac drives Hagar and her son into the wilderness, where God provides water.`,
      tour: `Genesis 21 contains two episodes separated by what looks like a simple editorial conjunction — after these things — but which are morally and emotionally complex in their relationship. The first is the long-promised birth of Isaac, and Sarah's declaration that God has made laughter for her and everyone who hears will laugh with her — the tone is joy. The second is the expulsion of Hagar and Ishmael. Sarah sees Ishmael laughing — the Hebrew tsahaq, the same root as Isaac's name, and possibly the same laughter — and insists to Abraham that the slave woman and her son must go. Abraham is greatly distressed. God tells him to listen to Sarah: through Isaac shall your offspring be named. But I will make the son of the slave woman also into a nation. Hagar is sent away with bread and a skin of water into the wilderness of Beersheba. When the water runs out, she cannot bear to watch the boy die. The angel of God calls to her and she sees a well. The two episodes together — laughter at Isaac's birth, water in the wilderness for Ishmael — insist that the covenantal line and the excluded line are both held by God.`,
