      title: "Genesis 38",
      tourTitle: "Judah and Tamar",
      hook: `Planted mid-way through the Joseph story, this chapter follows Judah's private failures — and Tamar's brilliant, risky justice.`,
      tour: `Genesis 38 interrupts the Joseph novella completely and follows Judah away from his brothers. He marries a Canaanite woman, fathers three sons — Er, Onan, and Shelah. Er is wicked and God kills him. Tamar, Er's wife, should pass to Onan under levirate custom. Onan knows the offspring will not be counted as his own, so he spills his semen on the ground to prevent Tamar from conceiving; God kills him too. Judah, fearing a third son's death, sends Tamar back to her father's house to wait for Shelah to grow up — but Shelah grows up and no marriage is offered. When Tamar learns that Judah will pass through her region, she covers herself with a veil, sits by the road, and is taken for a prostitute. Judah sleeps with her in exchange for a future payment; she keeps his signet, cord, and staff as pledges. When her pregnancy becomes apparent, Judah orders her burned for harlotry. She produces the pledges. He is more righteous than I, Judah says — she has been in the right, and I withheld my son from her. Tamar bears twins, Perez and Zerah. Perez is the ancestor of David. The chapter ends and the Joseph story resumes.`,
