      tourTitle: "The cupbearer and the baker",
      hook: `Two men dream in prison; Joseph interprets both correctly and asks to be remembered — and the cupbearer forgets him for two full years.`,
      tour: `Pharaoh's chief cupbearer and chief baker offend their master and are thrown into the same prison where Joseph is confined. They are assigned to Joseph's care. One morning he sees that they are troubled; they have each had a dream and there is no one to interpret. Joseph's response is theologically precise: interpretations belong to God, he says, and invites them to tell their dreams. The cupbearer dreams of a vine with three branches that blossomed, produced clusters, and were pressed into Pharaoh's cup, which the cupbearer held and gave to Pharaoh. Joseph interprets: three branches are three days; in three days Pharaoh will restore you to your position. He then asks: remember me when it goes well with you and mention me to Pharaoh and get me out of this place. The baker, encouraged, tells his dream: three baskets of white bread on his head, and birds eating from the uppermost basket. Joseph interprets: three baskets are three days; in three days Pharaoh will lift your head off you and hang you on a tree. Both interpretations prove exactly right. The cupbearer does not remember Joseph. He forgets him.`,
      blurb: `Joseph interprets the dreams of two fellow prisoners — one promises the cupbearer's restoration, one the baker's execution — both come true, but the cupbearer forgets Joseph for two years.`,
      summary: [
        `The prison episode extends Joseph's suspended state between the slave trade and his Egyptian ascent, and it introduces the motif that will carry the remainder of the novella: the interpretation of dreams. Two high officials of Pharaoh's household arrive in the prison, and Joseph is assigned as their attendant. When he finds them downcast one morning and they explain that they have each had a dream with no interpreter available, Joseph redirects the question: do not interpretations belong to God? The phrasing is important — it is the first time Joseph attributes his interpretive ability explicitly to God rather than claiming it as his own skill. The offer to help follows naturally.`,
        `The two interpretations are structurally symmetrical — three of something equals three days, the action is restoration or death, Pharaoh is the agent — but their outcomes are opposite. The cupbearer's dream is benign; the baker's is lethal. Joseph's request to the cupbearer — mention me to Pharaoh and get me out of here, for I was indeed stolen out of the land of the Hebrews — is the only moment in the novella where Joseph explicitly advocates for himself. It is also, the text implies, a minor lapse: he is trusting a human intermediary rather than waiting for God's timing. The cupbearer does not remember. The phrase 'two full years' that opens the next chapter underlines the duration of the forgetting and positions the timing of the final breakthrough as precisely calibrated.`,
      ],
