      tourTitle: "Pharaoh's dreams; Joseph as vizier",
      hook: `Seven fat cows, seven lean cows, seven plump ears of grain: Pharaoh dreams twice, and the man forgotten in prison becomes the lord of Egypt.`,
      tour: `Two years after the cupbearer's release, Pharaoh dreams twice in one night: seven fat cows devoured by seven thin cows, and seven plump ears of grain swallowed by seven thin ears. He summons all the magicians and wise men of Egypt and none can interpret. The cupbearer remembers Joseph and tells Pharaoh; Joseph is brought from the pit, shaved, clothed, and brought before Pharaoh. Joseph's first words are clarifying: it is not in me — God will give Pharaoh a favorable answer. He interprets both dreams as one: seven years of plenty followed by seven years of famine so severe that the plenty will be forgotten. Then he volunteers a policy solution without being asked: let Pharaoh appoint a discerning and wise man over Egypt to gather one-fifth of the produce during the seven years of plenty. Pharaoh turns to his servants: can we find anyone else in whom is the Spirit of God? He appoints Joseph, gives him his signet ring, a gold chain, linen robes, and the title Zaphenath-paneah. Joseph is thirty. He was seventeen when he was sold. The seven years of plenty proceed; Joseph stockpiles grain like the sand of the sea. His two sons are born before the famine arrives — Manasseh (God has made me forget my hardship) and Ephraim (God has made me fruitful). The famine begins.`,
