      title: "Genesis 45",
      tourTitle: "Joseph reveals himself",
      hook: `Joseph can no longer hold himself in — he clears the room, weeps so loudly the Egyptians hear, and says: I am Joseph. Is my father still alive?`,
      tour: `Genesis 45 is the recognition scene, and the narrative handles it with the same restraint it applied to the Aqedah in chapter 22 — almost nothing is said about what either party feels in the moment before everything changes. Joseph tells everyone to go out. He is left alone with his brothers. He weeps aloud; the Egyptians hear it in the next room. He says: I am Joseph. Is my father still alive? His brothers cannot answer; they are dismayed before him. He calls them closer. I am Joseph your brother, whom you sold into Egypt. Now do not be distressed or angry with yourselves — God sent me before you to preserve life. He reframes the whole story: the five remaining years of famine, God's plan to preserve a remnant, God who sent him here, not they. He tells them to bring their father. He falls on Benjamin's neck weeping; Benjamin weeps on his. He kisses all his brothers and weeps on them. Pharaoh hears, is pleased, and loads the brothers with wagons, provisions, and gifts. They leave. Jacob, told the news, does not believe — until he sees the wagons, and his spirit revives. He will go to see Joseph before he dies.`,
